### 2\. What the academic literature says

The literature points towards a useful way of thinking about OCR: treat it as a sequence of distinct problems, rather than one recognition problem.

A modern OCR pipeline typically has to solve five things:

* Image preparation: Is the text actually legible in the image? Is the page skewed, blurred, faded or unevenly illuminated?

* Layout detection: Where are the paragraphs, columns, tables, headings, captions and other elements?

* Text recognition: What characters and words are printed in each region?

* Reading order and structure: In what order should those regions be read? Which words belong in which table cells?

* Correction and validation: Are there recognition errors, omissions or implausible results that can be caught without changing what was actually printed?

This separation is important because each stage can fail independently. The OCR-D project, a major research and infrastructure initiative for evaluating OCR, specifically advocates evaluating text recognition, layout and reading order separately. GitHub +2

### A. Image preprocessing deserves more attention

This is probably the most obvious gap in Wiseau's current experiments.

There is a substantial research literature on document image quality assessment and restoration. A 2024 survey by Alaei and colleagues describes image quality assessment as an important early stage in document processing. It covers degradation such as noise, blur, poor contrast, uneven lighting, skew and other defects that interfere with recognition. SUNY University at Buffalo

A separate 2024 review of document binarisation examines methods for separating foreground text from the background. This matters particularly for old scans, where paper discolouration, fading and bleed-through can make the distinction between text and background difficult. MDPI +1

I'd investigate four fairly inexpensive transformations:

| Technique                             | What it does                                    | Why test it                                          |
| ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| Deskewing                             | Straightens tilted text                         | Improves line and column segmentation                |
| Contrast and background normalisation | Makes faded text more distinguishable           | Helps with old or unevenly scanned pages             |
| Adaptive thresholding (e.g. Sauvola)  | Separates text from locally varying backgrounds | Can help with yellowed paper and uneven illumination |
| Denoising                             | Reduces speckles and scanning artefacts         | May improve recognition of degraded scans            |

The important qualification is that preprocessing is not automatically beneficial. Aggressive thresholding can erase faint characters, punctuation and thin table borders. Denoising can remove precisely the small details OCR needs to recognise.

I would therefore avoid introducing a fixed preprocessing routine for every page. Instead, compare the original image with a small number of transformed versions and measure the results. Keep the original as a candidate.

My recommendation: build a small preprocessing experiment into your existing benchmark harness. You don't need a complicated machine-learning model to start. OpenCV can provide the transformations and some basic image-quality measurements.

### B. Layout detection is a separate discipline

Your recent work has established that page layout can be as consequential as character recognition.

The academic literature has increasingly treated document layout analysis as its own task. Rather than asking an OCR engine to infer everything from the page at once, specialist models identify regions and their relationships first.

One relevant dataset is DocLayNet. Published at KDD in 2022, it contains more than 80,000 manually annotated pages across 11 layout classes. It was designed to address the limited layout diversity of earlier datasets, many of which were dominated by scientific publications. doi.org +1

For Wiseau, the most relevant implementation to investigate is PaddleOCR's PP-StructureV3 / PP-DocLayout family.

These are not simply alternative text recognisers. They provide document layout detection, text extraction, table processing and reading-order reconstruction. PaddleOCR's documentation specifically describes handling complex multi-column documents and using cell detection to reconstruct tables. arxiv.org +2

I'd be interested in testing the layout component independently, even if you keep Tesseract as the text recogniser.

That would let you investigate a different architecture:

* Detect the page's distinct regions.

* Classify each region as text, table, heading, image or another relevant type.

* Crop or extract each region independently.

* Send it to the most appropriate recogniser.

* Reassemble the page using the detected layout and reading order.

This is a more flexible approach than trying to make one set of geometric rules work for every page.

### C. Table extraction has its own research ecosystem

This is particularly relevant given the remaining errors in your two-column tables and directory-style pages.

There's a useful distinction between recognising the text inside a table and reconstructing the table itself.

For example, a recogniser might identify every word in a table correctly but still fail to determine which words belong in which row or column.

The most relevant research here is Smock and colleagues' 2022 CVPR paper, PubTables-1M: Towards Comprehensive Table Extraction From Unstructured Documents.

It introduced a dataset of nearly one million tables with annotations for table boundaries, rows, columns, cells and headers. It also evaluates methods for detecting tables and reconstructing their internal structure. Microsoft Research +1

One resulting model worth trying is Microsoft's Table Transformer.

I wouldn't necessarily replace your current table-row stitching with it immediately. I'd run it as an independent table-structure detector on the pages where your current approach struggles.

In particular, I'd test:

* Two separate tables printed side by side.

* Tables with unusually narrow columns.

* Directory listings with no conventional grid lines.

* Tables where the first column contains short numbers or abbreviations.

* Tables with merged or multi-line cells.

The last two are important because geometric alignment alone can easily produce convincing but incorrect results.

### D. Vision-language models are not necessarily more reliable OCR engines

A recent survey, OCR in the Foundation Model Era: From Specialist Models to Unified Models, published on 3 November 2026, compares traditional OCR toolkits, specialist document vision-language models, hybrid approaches and general-purpose vision-language models. arxiv.org

One of its most important observations is that generative models can produce plausible-looking text that isn't actually present in the image.

This is especially problematic for tables. Models may omit rows, invent content, collapse columns or silently alter text to make it more coherent. Layout-guided, region-level recognition is one approach researchers are exploring to reduce these errors. arxiv.org +1

This reinforces something already apparent in your experiments: a model that produces more readable prose isn't necessarily producing more faithful OCR.

I would make the following distinction:

OCR should transcribe what is visible, not what the model believes the document ought to say.

For historical or archival material, preserve unusual spellings, apparent typographical errors, names and numbers. Don't automatically correct them.

There is research on domain-specific post-OCR correction. For example, Lyu and colleagues' 2021 paper, Neural OCR Post-Hoc Correction of Historical Corpora, demonstrated substantial improvements using a correction model trained on paired historical text. However, that approach depended on a large corpus of aligned OCR output and ground truth. It isn't equivalent to asking a general-purpose LLM to proofread a scan. doi.org +1

I'd regard post-correction as a possible later project, not the next priority.

### 3\. What I think you're missing

These are the things I'd prioritise, ranked by expected value against implementation effort.

### 1\. Run a proper preprocessing bake-off

I'd start here.

Create four versions of each test image:

* Original, unmodified.

* Deskewed and contrast-normalised.

* Adaptive thresholding (Sauvola).

* A lightly denoised version.

Run your current best OCR pipeline against each version. Compare results by page type and image quality.

This is a relatively inexpensive experiment that could reveal whether some of your remaining errors originate before recognition even begins.

I'd also record basic quality measures for each page: skew angle, contrast, blur and noise. Eventually, you could use these to decide whether preprocessing is needed at all.

### 2\. Try a specialist layout and table detector

My first experiment would be PaddleOCR's PP-StructureV3, particularly its layout detection and table recognition components.

I'd also try Microsoft's Table Transformer against your problematic pages.

There is a reason I'd prioritise these over yet another general OCR engine: your recent results suggest that layout reconstruction is an important remaining source of errors.

The literature supports treating layout, text recognition and table structure as separate tasks. It also provides established datasets and models for doing so.

