### 4\. I'd improve the evaluation methodology as well

I think your current benchmarking is one of Wiseau's strengths.

The fact that Set D had manually transcribed ground truth prepared before the engines were evaluated is good experimental practice. Freezing the new method before testing it also makes the results more meaningful.

However, I would make three changes.

### A. Expand beyond the current document family

Set D is a useful held-out test, but it consists of 12 pages drawn from two volumes of the same historical journal series.

That means it tests generalisation to new pages within a familiar document family. It doesn't yet establish generalisation to different publications, printing conventions or scan conditions.

I'd create Set E using entirely different source documents.

Include examples of:

* Different historical publications and fonts.

* Poorer-quality scans.

* Skewed and unevenly illuminated pages.

* Multiple tables on a page.

* Pages with unusual column arrangements.

* Documents with embedded or hidden OCR layers.

Keep the ground truth blind and frozen before evaluating new methods.

### B. Separate recognition accuracy from layout accuracy

WER is useful, but it can conflate two different problems.

Suppose OCR correctly recognises every word in a two-column page but reads the right column before the left.

The words themselves are correct, but the output sequence is wrong. WER will penalise that.

I'd retain your existing metrics and add:

| Metric                     | What it tells you                                                 |
| -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Word error rate (WER)      | How many words are wrong, missing or incorrectly inserted         |
| Character error rate (CER) | How accurately individual characters were recognised              |
| Bag-of-words error         | Whether the words are correct, regardless of their order          |
| Reading-order accuracy     | Whether blocks appear in the correct sequence                     |
| Table structure accuracy   | Whether rows, columns and cells have been reconstructed correctly |
| Numeric recall             | Whether numbers have been preserved                               |

For table structure, investigate TEDS (Tree Edit Distance-based Similarity), a metric used to compare reconstructed table structures. OmniDocBench is another useful reference for evaluating document parsing beyond simple transcription. GitHub +2

This would help you identify whether a particular improvement comes from better character recognition or better reconstruction of the page.

